● The Terminal, Reconsidered

  The terminal is the oldest interface in computing still in daily use, and its persistence is not
  an accident of nostalgia but a testament to a genuinely good design: a shared, composable text
  channel between human intent and machine execution. Where graphical interfaces ask designers to
  anticipate every action a user might take and build a button or menu for it, the terminal asks
  only that actions be expressible as text. This is a much lower bar, and it is why the terminal
  remains the substrate on which so much of modern software creation happens, from compiling code to
  orchestrating cloud infrastructure to, now, conversing with AI agents.

  What makes the terminal durable is composability. A shell like bash treats every program as a
  black box that reads text and writes text, and the pipe operator lets you chain these boxes into
  structures nobody designed in advance. grep, sort, and `w
